==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|-}} {{suffix|en|noy|some}} (short for [[annoy]], from {{m|enm|annoien|an(n)oien}}, {{m|enm|enoien}} from {{der|en|xno|-}} {{m|fro|anuier}}, from {{der|en|fro|enuier}} ({{cog|fr|ennuyer}}), from {{der|en|LL.|inodiare||to make hateful}}, from {{m|la|in-|pos=intensive prefix}} + {{m|la|odium||hate}}.<ref>[http://wordsmith.org/words/noisome.html Wordsmith's entry for noisome]</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈnɔɪ.səm/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔɪsəm|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|literary}} Morally [[hurtful]] or [[noxious]].
# {{lb|en|literary}} Hurtful or noxious to health; [[unwholesome]], [[insalubrious]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:harmful}}
#* {{quote-book
|lang=en
|year=1912
|author=Alexander Berkman
|authorlink=Alexander Berkman
|title=Prison Memoirs of an Anarchist
|chapter=6
|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:Prison_Memoirs_of_an_Anarchist/Part_I/Chapter_6}}
|passage=There is a distinct sense of freedom in the solitude of the night. The day's atmosphere is surcharged with '''noisome''' anxiety, the hours laden with impending terrors. But the night is soothing.}}
# {{lb|en|literary}} Offensive to the senses; [[disgusting]], [[unpleasant]], [[nauseous]], especially having an undesirable [[smell]]
#: {{syn|en|foul|fetid|sickening|nauseating}}

